# Solution — GIAM 1.5 Problem 3

## Problem

Formulate a description of the $ \gcd $ of two numbers in terms of their prime factorizations in the general case (when the factorizations may include powers of the primes involved).

## The Description

Write each number using the **same list of primes** $ p_1, p_2, \dots, p_k $ — every prime that divides *either* number — allowing exponent $ 0 $ for a prime that is absent:

$$$
a = p_1^{e_1} p_2^{e_2} \cdots p_k^{e_k}, \qquad b = p_1^{f_1} p_2^{f_2} \cdots p_k^{f_k}, \qquad e_i, f_i \ge 0.
$$$

Then the greatest common divisor takes, for each prime, the **smaller** of the two exponents:

$$$
\boxed{\;\gcd(a,b) = p_1^{\min(e_1,f_1)} \, p_2^{\min(e_2,f_2)} \cdots p_k^{\min(e_k,f_k)} = \prod_{i=1}^{k} p_i^{\min(e_i,\,f_i)}.\;}
$$$

In words: **the gcd is the product over all primes of that prime raised to the smaller of its two exponents.**

## Why the Minimum

A number $ d = \prod_i p_i^{g_i} $ **divides** $ a $ exactly when its exponent on each prime does not exceed $ a $'s — that is, $ g_i \le e_i $ for every $ i $ (this is what unique factorization buys us). For $ d $ to be a *common* divisor of $ a $ and $ b $ it must divide both, so

$$$
g_i \le e_i \quad\text{and}\quad g_i \le f_i, \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad g_i \le \min(e_i, f_i).
$$$

The **greatest** such $ d $ makes every exponent as large as allowed, namely $ g_i = \min(e_i, f_i) $. That is precisely the boxed formula.

## A Worked Example

Take $ a = 2^3\cdot 3\cdot 5^2 $ and $ b = 2\cdot 3^4\cdot 7 $. Listing both over the primes $ \{2,3,5,7\} $ (padding absent primes with exponent $ 0 $) and taking the smaller exponent in each column:

!(96%)[Aligning two factorizations over the same primes and taking the minimum exponent in each column](problem-3-mingcd.svg "Both numbers are written over the same primes 2,3,5,7, padding an absent prime with exponent 0. For each prime the gcd takes the minimum of the two exponents: min(3,1)=1 for 2, min(1,4)=1 for 3, min(2,0)=0 for 5, min(0,1)=0 for 7. So gcd = 2·3 = 6. The lcm uses max instead, and min+max = sum gives gcd·lcm = ab.")

$$$
\gcd(a,b) = 2^{\min(3,1)}\,3^{\min(1,4)}\,5^{\min(2,0)}\,7^{\min(0,1)} = 2^1\,3^1\,5^0\,7^0 = 6.
$$$

## Remark — Padding With Zero Exponents Is Essential

The phrase "the same list of primes" does the real work. A prime that appears in only one number is included in *both* factorizations with exponent $ 0 $ in the one it is missing from. Since $ \min(e,0) = 0 $, such a prime contributes $ p^0 = 1 $ to the $ \gcd $ — it correctly drops out. Without this padding convention the "minimum over each prime" rule would not be well defined, because the two products would range over different primes. This is exactly why, in Problem 2, the coprime pair $ 110 = 2\cdot 5\cdot 11 $ and $ 273 = 3\cdot 7\cdot 13 $ had $ \gcd = 1 $: *every* prime is missing from one of them, so every exponent-minimum is $ 0 $.

## Remark — The Companion lcm Formula and the Product Identity

The same setup gives the least common multiple by swapping $ \min $ for $ \max $:

$$$
\operatorname{lcm}(a,b) = \prod_{i=1}^{k} p_i^{\max(e_i,\,f_i)}.
$$$

Because, for any two numbers, $ \min(e,f) + \max(e,f) = e + f $, multiplying the two formulas prime-by-prime gives

$$$
\gcd(a,b)\cdot\operatorname{lcm}(a,b) = \prod_i p_i^{\,\min(e_i,f_i) + \max(e_i,f_i)} = \prod_i p_i^{\,e_i + f_i} = a\,b.
$$$

So the single identity $ \gcd\cdot\operatorname{lcm} = ab $ used numerically in Problem 2 is just this exponent fact in disguise.

## Remark — It Rests on Unique Factorization

This whole description is only meaningful because each number has *exactly one* prime factorization (up to order) — the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic. The exponents $ e_i, f_i $ are then unambiguous, the "divides $ \iff $ exponent-wise $ \le $" criterion holds, and the $ \gcd $ is pinned down uniquely. The formula also extends verbatim to more than two numbers: $ \gcd(a,b,c,\dots) = \prod_i p_i^{\min(e_i,f_i,g_i,\dots)} $, taking the minimum exponent across *all* the numbers.

## Remark — Conceptual Clarity vs. Practical Computation

This min-of-exponents description is the clearest *conceptual* picture of what a $ \gcd $ is, and it makes facts like $ \gcd\cdot\operatorname{lcm}=ab $ transparent. It is not, however, how one usually *computes* a $ \gcd $: it presupposes the prime factorizations, and factoring large numbers is hard. Euclid's algorithm (Problem 2) finds the same $ \gcd $ without ever factoring. The two viewpoints are complementary — the prime-factorization formula tells us *what* the $ \gcd $ is, while Euclid tells us *how* to get it quickly.
